fix(pip): Bind the lens pass to its own exposure, and refuse HDR10 lens publishing

Found by auditing every resource the lens material binds instead of one symptom at a time.

$user$tonemap is the luminance chain output and the lens shader multiplies the second view by
it (models_scope_reticle.ps reads s_tonemap.Load(int3(0,0,0)).x twice). rt_LUM_pool is created
per target set, but phase_combine bound t_LUM_src/t_LUM_dest through an "already bound"
comparison against a name keyed texture object, which is stale as soon as the active set
changes: the lens pass could tone map its image with the main set s exposure. That is the dark
lens the dumps show, the same scene correctly exposed in the main view and about 2.5x darker
inside the sight. The pool is now bound to this set on every pass, and the legacy code s
if/else with two identical branches - an attempt at exactly this split that was never written
out - is gone.

The audit also found that publishing the lens image uses CopyResource into $user$viewport2,
which Direct3D only performs when both textures share a format: rt_Generic_0 is A16B16G16R16F
under HDR10 while rt_secondVP is A2R10G10B10, so under HDR10 the copy would fail silently and
the lens would hold a stale image. The second set is now refused with a message in that
configuration instead of half working.

Thermal audit: the pack marks thermal sights with scope_lense_thermal on the weapon child
sections (wpn_*_gauss_sight) and on the token sections (gauss, gauss_sight); echo1 is not
marked, so the sessions that reported no thermal effect never aimed a marked thermal optic -
the log only ever shows wpn_m82 and wpn_rsass_echo1. ps_r2_heatvision is the variant index of
s_heatvision and only E[1] is ever drawn, so mapping the pack s mode to 1 is correct.